-- Repro: ALTER SETTINGS wrote a qualified name into the model without resolving
-- it. Both statements below reported success, `mxcli check --references` said
-- "All references valid", and mxbuild answered one build later:
--
--   [error] [CE1613] "The selected microflow 'Nope.MF_DoesNotExist' no longer
--                     exists." at Runtime settings
--   [error] [CE1613] "The selected constant 'Nope.AlsoMissing' no longer
--                     exists." at Configuration 'Default'
--
-- The constant is the worse of the two: the override is CREATED for a constant
-- that does not exist, so a typo leaves dead configuration data behind rather
-- than failing — the "no silent side effects on typos" item of the PR checklist.
--
-- Settings were the only statement family that resolved nothing, which is why
-- this survived: every other family runs its references through the same pass.
--
-- Expected: both statements refused, by `mxcli check --references` AND by exec
-- (the guard is in the handler, so `--no-check` does not get around it).
--
--   mxcli check 274-settings-reference-validation.mdl -p app.mpr --references
--
-- NOT named .fail.mdl, deliberately. `make check-mdl` runs `mxcli check` WITHOUT
-- a project, and this rule cannot decide anything without one — it resolves the
-- names against the model. As valid MDL the file checks clean there, so naming
-- it .fail.mdl reports "negative test unexpectedly passed" and makes a working
-- rule look regressed (the Makefile says so above check-mdl; #891/#892 are the
-- precedents). The guard is covered by unit tests in
-- mdl/executor/validate_settings_refs_test.go instead.
--
-- The controls that matter are in 274-settings-reference-valid.mdl: the same
-- settings, with the microflow and constant created earlier in the same script,
-- must still pass — a check that rejects everything is not a check.

alter settings model AfterStartupMicroflow = 'Nope.MF_DoesNotExist';
alter settings constant 'Nope.AlsoMissing' value 'x' in configuration 'Default';
